When you use paweli to compile a program, paweli may combine its own
runtime, the VM, garbage collector, and standard library, with your
compiled program. This happens whenever `paweli build` produces a
`.paw` bytecode container, and especially with `--bin`, which embeds a
full copy of the paweli binary into the resulting native executable.
The purpose of this Exception is to allow programs written in the
paweli language, including non-GPL and proprietary ones, to be
compiled and distributed this way without the paweli Runtime's GPLv3
license extending to them.

0. Definitions.

A file is an "Independent Module" if it is written in the paweli
language and either requires the Runtime for execution after a
Compilation Process, or makes use of an interface provided by the
Runtime, but is not otherwise based on the Runtime.

"paweli" means a version of the paweli compiler and runtime, with or
without modifications, governed by version 3 (or a specified later
version) of the GNU General Public License (GPL) with the option of
using any subsequent versions published by the FSF.

"GPL-compatible Software" is software whose conditions of
propagation, modification and use would permit combination with
paweli in accord with the license of paweli.

"Target Code" refers to output from the paweli compiler: a `.paw`
bytecode container, or a native executable produced with `--bin`,
either suitable for execution by the paweli runtime.

The "Compilation Process" transforms a `.pw` source file, written in
the paweli language, into Target Code. Thus, for example, use of
source generators or preprocessors need not be considered part of the
Compilation Process, since the Compilation Process can be understood
as starting with the output of the generators or preprocessors.

A Compilation Process is "Eligible" if it is done using paweli, alone
or with other GPL-compatible software, or if it is done without using
any work based on paweli. For example, using non-GPL-compatible
software to modify paweli's own compiled bytecode output before it
reaches its final Target Code form would not qualify as an Eligible
Compilation Process.

1. Grant of Additional Permission.

You have permission to propagate a work of Target Code formed by
combining the Runtime with Independent Modules, even if such
propagation would otherwise violate the terms of GPLv3, provided that
all Target Code was generated by Eligible Compilation Processes. You
may then convey such a combination under terms of your choice,
consistent with the licensing of the Independent Modules.

